    "final_assistant_text": "Both changes are complete and verified by reading the files back.\n\n**1. `src/pipeline/steps.py`**\n```python\ndef normalize_step(step: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the canonical form of a step name: trimmed, lowercased, and\n    with leading and trailing slash characters removed.\"\"\"\n    return step.strip().strip(\"/\").strip().lower()\n```\n- `normalize_step(\" /Deploy /\")` → `\"deploy\"` (whitespace trimmed, surrounding slashes removed, lowercased)\n- `normalize_step(\"a/b\")` stays `\"a/b\"` (only leading/trailing slashes removed)\n\n**2. `src/pipeline/runner.py`**\n```python\nfrom .steps import normalize_step\n\n\ndef run_step(step: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Run one step after cleaning its name with the canonical rule.\"\"\"\n    cleaned = normalize_step(step)\n    return f\"ran {cleaned}\"\n```\nThe inline `step.strip().lower()` copy is gone, and `run_step` now delegates to `normalize_step`, so the rule lives in exactly one place.\n\nI did not modify `README.md` or anything under `tests/`.\n\nOne honest caveat: this environment's policy blocks command execution, so I couldn't run the provided unittest command. I verified the changes by reading both files back and tracing the specified examples through the new implementation; the logic produces `\"deploy\"` for `\" /Deploy /\"` and preserves interior slashes in `\"a/b\"`.",
